  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA stacked low pressure system situated north of the Great Lakes\nwill weaken and lift northeastward today while surface high\npressure slowly works overhead by Tuesday morning. Light\nnorthwest inflow around the building high will maintain\ncomfortable humidity levels and slightly below-normal\ntemperatures today. A few light showers across northern West \nVirginia will exit early this morning as mid-level clouds clear \nfrom southwest to northeast. \n\nAs surface high pressure settles directly overhead tonight and\nupper-level flow flattens, light winds and clear skies will\npromote strong radiational cooling. River valley fog is \nexpected to develop after midnight. Another round of valley fog \nis expected late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning under \nlight synoptic flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBy Thursday morning, low pressure moving north of the Great\nLakes will extend a cold front across the lower Ohio Valley and\ninto the southern Plains. Low-level moisture pooling ahead of\nthe boundary will push surface dewpoints back into the lower 60s\nacross the lowlands, supporting modest surface-based \ninstability. Deep-layer vertical shear of 25 to 30KTs will be \nsufficient for updraft storm organization. \n\nGiven a dry mid-level layer between H850 and H700 and steepening\nlapse rates, downdraft momentum transport and hail survival will\nbe enhanced, supporting a threat for localized damaging wind\ngusts and hail. At this point it seems like the most likely\nlocation for this would be across the middle Ohio Valley and\npoints west (largely keeping the threat out of us). However, \nthe exact location and timing of the highest instability axis \nremain unclear.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA secondary cold front is scheduled to cross the region on\nFriday. Dynamic lift and lingering moisture beneath the trough\naxis will support scattered showers and isolated non-severe\nthunderstorms, primarily focused during peak afternoon heating. \n\nIn the wake of Friday's boundary, high zonal flow will \noverspread the region through the weekend. High pressure sliding\nacross the Great Lakes will bring a return of dry conditions, \nlight surface winds, and near-seasonal temperatures.",
